{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m applying the POSE review workflow, then I’ll inspect the sealed spec and diff, run the required engine checks through `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, and verify the runtime changes with focused tests. I’ll keep the checkout read-only and account for all round 10 findings in the draft."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .agents/skills/pose-review/SKILL.md && printf '\\\\n--- SPEC ---\\\\n' && cat .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md && printf '\\\\n--- ENV ---\\\\n' && printenv POSE_REVIEW_CLI && printf '\\\\n--- STATUS ---\\\\n' && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .agents/skills/pose-review/SKILL.md && printf '\\\\n--- SPEC ---\\\\n' && cat .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md && printf '\\\\n--- ENV ---\\\\n' && printenv POSE_REVIEW_CLI && printf '\\\\n--- STATUS ---\\\\n' && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: pose-review\ndescription: Use for POSE pull-request or code review to verify controlled scope, preserved contracts, security and observability impact, risk-proportional validation, and escalation where applicable. Trigger keywords - review, code review, PR review, review opinion, ultrareview.\nwhen_to_use: Evaluating your own or another author's diff or PR under POSE. Use before commenting or approving to select rules, inspect validation evidence and prior decisions, and issue an actionable decision.\npose_schema_range: \"1-1\"\nclients: agents-skills, mcp, claude-code\ncapabilities: read\n---\n\n# Skill: pose-review\n\n## Required reading\n\n1. [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md).\n2. [`.pose/workflows/review.md`](../../../.pose/workflows/review.md).\n3. Applicable domain rules; security takes precedence in conflicts.\n\nResolve the selected task with `pose context --task <typed-or-qualified-artifact-ref> --json` before reviewing. Follow its canonical authority and stop on ambiguity, stale binding, unsupported metadata or `transfer-in-progress`. Use the qualified `xref:` scope for cross-project review; every cross-project write requires an explicit `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding and the current `context_revision` as `--expect-context`. Keep review evidence and its `POSE-Spec:` trailer in the authority repository.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Classify the change as feature, bugfix, refactor, documentation, or mixed.\n2. Run `pose assess tech-debt` during code review to ensure markers (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `stub`, `panic`) are covered by follow-ups or specs. Run `pose assess integrate` when touching inter-module contracts (Protobuf, Kafka, REST, MCP).\n3. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` to inspect effective criteria, components and tool requirements.\n4. Run active required tools first, record why each recommended tool was used or skipped, and keep completion tools deferred until attestation.\n5. Select rules with `pose suggest review --path <affected-dir>` for every mapped component.\n6. Search `.pose/knowledge/` for prior module decisions, accepted risks, and pending follow-ups.\n7. Require deterministic `pose validate --strict` evidence matching delivery targets.\n8. Evaluate every required plan criterion, including cross-component boundaries. When the change adds or alters a governance gate, also answer: what is the cheapest way to satisfy this gate in form without producing its value, and what stops that? Point to the adversarial corpus case that proves it (`pose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md`), or record the gap.\n9. Classify findings as critical, high, medium, or low with evidence and expected action.\n10. Run `pose recurrence-check --tolerant --window-days 14`; use recurrence escalation for a matching systemic signal.\n11. Create a handoff with `pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>` for accepted residual risk, monitoring, or deferred action.\n12. Seal the review subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`.\n13. Prepare the mechanical half with `pose review auto-attest <bundle-id> --reviewer agent:<id>` — without `--apply` — and read `review_attestation.pending`: those are the criteria no registered check answers, and they are yours to answer. Then record the attestation with `pose review attest`, giving every judgment criterion a conclusion. `--apply` on auto-attest records only when nothing is pending; it refuses otherwise, and never answers a judgment criterion for you. An explicit attestation records `--criterion ID|passed|evidence` (or `not-applicable` with a rationale, or `finding` naming a recorded finding) and `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]`; every finding needs a severity and an action, and an `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` finding also needs an owner, a rationale and a review date. A `passed` criterion may cite only evidence the sealed bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts. For a cross-project attestation, use the qualified `xref:` as the target argument to `pose review attest`, include `--expect-context <digest>` with `--apply`, and keep the explicit project binding; a bundle ID alone does not select an authority project.\n14. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; close only when both confirm a valid, approved attestation.\n15. Decide: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n16. When Contributor Mode is active, if review uncovers POSE linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal with `pose contribute stage --type enhancement --title \"<summary>\"`.\n\n## Output requirements\n\n- Completed Rules applied during review section.\n- Bundle/plan digests and dispositions for required, recommended and deferred completion tools.\n- Severity-classified findings with expected actions.\n- Clear and actionable final decision.\n- Handoff when residual risk is accepted.\n\n--- SPEC ---\n---\nslug: pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes\nstatus: in-progress        # draft | in-progress | done | superseded | abandoned; blocked = operational wait, not terminal\ncreated_at: 2026-11-23\ncompleted_at:        # stamped on the transition to status: done\nsupersedes:          # slug of the superseded spec (when applicable)\ndepends_on:          # prerequisites, inline list: other-spec, milestone:<roadmap>/<id>, roadmap:<slug>\nremediates:          # optional explicit links: spec:slug@defect-fix, finding:rva-<16hex>/F1@revert (never inferred from depends_on)\npriority: 2\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: bugfix\nsurface: standard\ndelivers: capability:review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nKeep a delegated review run concludable when the reviewer ends while a process it started is still running, by terminating and reaping the run's remaining processes before the cleanup inventory.\n\n### Business value\nRound 2 of `pose-review-readiness-before-seal` (rrn-9d5816c780952f13) produced a complete draft with two valid findings, but its cleanup was `runtime cleanup inventory incomplete; resources retained` because a broad `go test` the reviewer started was still running. The run could not be concluded and its findings had to be carried by hand. On 2026-11-24 a dispatch stopped during setup left reservation `000002.json` of attempt `rat-cfa14ceb…` with no run: every later dispatch failed with `attempt … incomplete or unreadable`, and `pose delegate cancel` refused it as `stalled, not running`; the reservation had to be moved out of the repository by hand.\n\n### Constraints\nCleanup keeps proving that the sealed checkout is unchanged and that no owned resource survives; only the order changes. A run whose resources truly cannot be reclaimed stays unconcludable.\n\n### Non-goals\nChanging review independence, round accounting or the attestation preflight.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: When the adapter exits, the dispatch shall terminate every remaining process of the run's process group, wait for them within a bound, and only then take the cleanup inventory.\n- R2: A run whose leftover processes were terminated and reaped, and whose inventory then completes, shall be concludable; the run record shall say which processes were terminated.\n- R3: The review brief shall ask the reviewer to let every command it starts finish before ending.\n- R4: A regression test shall dispatch an adapter that leaves a background process running and conclude the run.\n- R5: An attempt reservation whose dispatch was interrupted before its run was recorded shall not block later dispatches: `pose delegate ls` shall show it as abandoned with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel` (or the next dispatch) shall release it with a record, never by deleting the reservation.\n- R6: A regression test shall interrupt a dispatch between the reservation and the run record and dispatch again.\n\n### Non-functional\nExisting behaviour outside the stated requirements is unchanged.\n\n### Security\nNo new input crosses a trust boundary without the existing confinement.\n\n### Compatibility\nRecords written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n`pose-mcp` (review dispatch and conclusion), the manual and its embedded copy.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes.md\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim_linux.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/main_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/engine_reads_raw_history_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/cli.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/cli_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_list.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_attempt_ledger.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_adapter_containment_regression_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief_conclusion_rules_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/help_catalog.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate.go\n\n\n### API/contract changes\n`pose delegate cancel` releases a stalled attempt that has no run, recording a failed run and its receipt. Brief template `review-brief/10`. The run record's `safety.limitations` names the processes the dispatch ended after the adapter.\n\n\n### Data/storage changes\nA released attempt gets an ordinary failed run record and attempt receipt; no schema changes.\n\n\n### Technical risks\nOn Linux the pose command runs the adapter under the review run shim, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree and ends what the run leaves in it after the adapter exits, on a timeout and when the dispatcher ends; the subtree is the run's by construction. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to the search by the sealed-checkout environment entry, which a reviewer process can clear or set (L6). Outside Linux only the process-group kill applies (L2).\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Planning\n- [ ] Confirm the cause against the current engine before starting.\n\n### Implementation\n- [ ] Implement each requirement with a test that fails before it.\n\n### Validation\n- [ ] `pose validate --carry` and `pose check --strict`.\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Basis: R1, R2\nThe run's leftovers are identified by the process tree, not by the environment: on Linux the adapter runs under a shim that is the subreaper of the run's subtree, so every process the run starts, including one that opens its own session or clears its environment, stays the shim's to end. A subreaper in the dispatcher itself was rejected because it is process-wide there and adopted children other code started in the same process. The sealed-checkout entry the dispatch already sets remains a fallback for an embedder without the shim.\n\n### Decision D2\n- Basis: R5\nAn abandoned attempt is closed by the records a failed run writes (a run record and its receipt), released only when its marker is stalled and its owner proven dead. Nothing is deleted from the ledger, and older engines read the result as an ordinary failed run.\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nEach requirement gets a regression test shown failing before its code.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./...` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --carry`.\n\n### Execution log\n- 2026-11-24: implemented R1–R6. Each has a test that fails with its fix mutated out: the dispatch ends a process the adapter left in a session of its own (the test's leftover keeps writing into the runtime directory), the release records the failed run and receipt and removes the marker and its stall claim, a live owner is never released, and `review-brief/10` asks commands to finish while `/9` stays unchanged.\n- 2026-11-24: the containment record disclosed escaped-descendant termination as `unavailable`, and a regression test pinned that limitation. On Linux it is now `environment-marked` (ended while the descendant keeps the run's sealed-checkout entry, never `available`, so a profile that requires the full guarantee is still refused), the limitation text says so, and the test now proves the escaped child is ended with the run.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 1 (rrn-c5c082703d83c834) requested changes. N1: the reservation was published before the active marker, so a dispatch ended in between left an attempt nothing could release; the dispatch now marks the attempt active first and publishes the reservation after, and a stalled marker without a reservation is removed by the release. When marking itself fails, the dispatch still publishes the reservation and records the failed run and its receipt, keeping the contract of `pose-delegation-result-before-marker`. N2: `delegate ls` now names the abandonment cause and the release command, in text and JSON. N3: each process is pinned by a pidfd and its environment read again through it before SIGKILL, so a reused pid is never signalled. N4: a scan that cannot list `/proc` or read a process state fails; a process of this user whose environment cannot be read is counted and disclosed in the run record rather than skipped (L3). N5: the manual says the reaping is Linux-only. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out (N4's disclosure is not reproducible in a test: it needs a non-inspectable process).\n- 2026-11-24: review round 2 (rrn-2c9fa8ac47c2fbab) requested changes. N1: a reservation an earlier engine left without any marker is now listed by `delegate ls` and released by `cancel` once it has no marker, receipt or run and is older than ten minutes (an earlier engine marked within moments of reserving). N2: a non-inspectable process of this user that did not exist before the adapter started may be the run's, so the scan fails and the run stays unconcludable; ones that predate the run are skipped and disclosed. N3: a release claims the attempt exclusively, so two cancels never record two runs. N4: `TestAnInterruptedDispatchIsReleasedAndTheNextOneRuns` interrupts a real dispatch after its reservation, sees the next dispatch refused with the release command, releases it and dispatches again. N5: a receipt that cannot be inspected is reported with its error. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out, except N5.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 3 (rrn-667b69e8c01c345c) requested changes. N1: the release claim records its owner; a claim whose owner is gone is taken over, and a release interrupted after recording its run completes with that run instead of recording another. N2: age proves nothing about a markerless reservation, so it is released only with `--confirm-abandoned`, the operator's assertion that no dispatch of it runs, which the recorded run states. N3: reservations and receipts that cannot be read are reported as losses by `delegate ls`. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 4 (rrn-97acfcc2809e32c2) requested changes. N1: `pose delegate cancel` now asks the release for every attempt that is not running, so a release interrupted after recording its run is completed even when the attempt already shows that run; an abandonment run no longer counts as the attempt's run when looking for orphans. N2: a stale claim is taken over by renaming it, which only one taker wins, then an exclusive publish. N3: with the run history incomplete, the release refuses to rule a run out and the listing reports why. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 5 (rrn-146bc70da7364cde) requested changes. N1: the published claim and its takeover protocol are replaced by an operating-system lock (`flock`) on a per-attempt lock file, which the kernel releases when its holder ends, so there is nothing to take over and no window between two takers. N2: a release that finds the receipt already published and the marker still stalled completes by removing the marker. N3: the release completes with any run the attempt already recorded (by its dispatch or by an earlier release), never writing a second one. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 6 (rrn-adf91bb46841bd66) requested changes. N1: an unreadable active marker may be a live attempt's, so no reservation is then taken as markerless. N2: a descendant that leaves the group, starts its own session and clears its environment is now ended too on Linux: the dispatcher makes itself a child subreaper, and after the adapter exits it ends every child reparented to it in another session, started after the adapter, that does not name another run's checkout, pinning each by pidfd and reaping it. N3: the disclosed count holds only processes that predate the run. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 7 (rrn-9abc2fe5dedc7478) requested changes. N1, N2: a subreaper that cannot be enabled or a run start that cannot be read now fails the cleanup instead of skipping the orphan scan. N3: a process start that cannot be read, in the baseline or the orphan scan, is an error, and a process absent from the baseline is never taken as preexisting. N4: the run record names the ended processes by pid only; their names, which the reviewer chooses, are not recorded. N4 has a test; N1–N3 are error paths the host cannot be made to produce in a test.\n- 2026-11-24: the matrix of round 8 failed `TestReviewDispatchRunsAndRecordsAdapters`, and the cause was the design, not the test: a subreaper is process-wide, so the dispatcher also adopted and ended children other code started in the same process (a detached delegation) and could reap a process another goroutine waited for. The subreaper moved into a review run shim: the dispatcher starts the adapter as `<pose> __pose-review-run-shim <adapter path> <argv>`, the shim makes itself the subreaper, runs the adapter as its only child, ends and reaps everything left in its subtree, reports the ended pids in a file the dispatcher reads, and returns the adapter's status, re-raising its signal so a lost process still reads as lost. The shim has no other children, so all it adopts is the run's. The pose command and the test binaries that dispatch enable it; another embedder starts the adapter directly. `TestAnOrphanThatClearedItsEnvironmentIsEnded` fails with the shim's reaping removed.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 8 (rrn-f19179878d867e9a) requested changes. N1: the environment entry is the reviewer's to set, so with the shim the dispatcher no longer searches by it; the shim's subtree is the run's by construction, and the search remains only for an embedder without the shim (L6). N2: the shim's report goes through a pipe passed as file descriptor 3, which the shim closes on exec, never through a path the reviewer can write. N3: a timeout sends the shim SIGTERM, and the shim ends the run's subtree before it exits; the group kill after the run stays the backstop. N4: the shim receives SIGTERM when its dispatcher ends (`PR_SET_PDEATHSIG`, checked against the dispatcher's pid at start) and ends the run, so a dead dispatcher never leaves a reviewer running. Each fix has a test that fails with it mutated out.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 9 (rrn-5bbd458c7701af31) requested changes. N1 (follow-up): the manual and the recorded limitation now describe the shim as the Linux path and the environment search as the fallback of an embedder without it. N2: the shim's report is bounded (at most 1000 pids, with the total counted, and a bounded error) and the dispatcher drains whatever exceeds its read, so a large report never blocks the shim. N3: a process the shim cannot inspect or end no longer stops the reaping of the others; the problems are reported when the bound expires. N1 and N2 have tests that fail with them mutated out; N3 needs an uninspectable child, which the test host cannot produce.\n- 2026-11-24: review round 10 (rrn-ecfc61a1c2b009f6) requested changes. N1: the shim no longer counts a process of another user whose stat it cannot read (procfs mounted hidepid) as a possible run child; only an unreadable process of this user is. N2 (follow-up): the orphan scan lists each directory with a bound and reports a cut listing as a loss. N3 (follow-up): the shim reports a pid only once its signal was delivered, and its error is recorded in the run's limitations. N4 (follow-up): Technical risks, D1 and the fallback helper's comment describe the shim as the Linux path. N2 has a test; N1 and N3 need a hidepid procfs or an unkillable child.\n\n\n### Results summary\nThe full `pose-mcp` suite passes; the canonical result is refreshed with `pose validate --carry`.\n\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning, test:TestATimeoutEndsTheRunsOrphans, test:TestTheShimEndsTheRunWhenItsDispatcherEnds, test:TestTheAdapterDoesNotInheritTheShimReport, test:TestTheShimReportIsBounded, test:TestAnOrphanThatClearedItsEnvironmentIsEnded, test:TestAPidThatLostTheRunsEntryIsNotKilled, test:TestANonInspectableProcessStartedDuringTheRunFailsTheScan\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestTheCurrentBriefAsksCommandsToFinish\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning\n- R5 [satisfied] test:TestAnAbandonedAttemptIsReleasedWithARecord, test:TestTheAttemptIsMarkedActiveBeforeItIsReserved, test:TestAStalledMarkerWithoutReservationIsListedAndRemoved\n- R6 [satisfied] test:TestAnInterruptedDispatchIsReleasedAndTheNextOneRuns, test:TestAMarkerlessReservationOfAnEarlierEngineIsReleased, test:TestOneReleaseRunsAtATime, test:TestARecordedRunCompletesItsAttempt, test:TestAnInterruptedReleaseCompletesWithItsRun, test:TestAnUnreadableReservationIsReportedAsALoss, test:TestAnIncompleteRunHistoryRulesNothingOut, test:TestAnUnreadableMarkerRulesNothingOut, test:TestTheOrphanScanListsBoundedDirectories\n\n\n### Known gaps\nThe release is driven through the Store, including after a real interrupted dispatch; the thin `pose delegate cancel` branch that calls it is not driven through the CLI, because a stalled marker there needs a dispatch process killed and three silent heartbeat intervals. N5 (the receipt inspection error) has no test: making the receipt path uninspectable also makes the reservation unreadable first.\n\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\nThe dispatch ends the processes a run left running before its cleanup and names them in the run record; `pose delegate cancel` releases an attempt abandoned before its run was recorded; `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish.\n\n\n### Files and modules changed\n`pose-mcp` (review dispatch, attempt ledger, delegation CLI, brief), the manual in both locales and its embedded copies.\n\n\n### Validation executed\n`go test ./...` in `pose-mcp`; `pose check --strict`; `pose validate --carry --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json`.\n\n\n### Residual risks\nL1: on Linux a run's process escapes only while it keeps a live parent outside the run and neither the run's group nor its environment entry, for example one handed to a service manager; the cleanup inventory still fails a run whose files it keeps changing. L2: outside Linux only the process-group kill applies. L6: an embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to the search by the sealed-checkout entry, which a reviewer process can set. L4: a reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner, so its release rests on the operator's confirmation, not on proof. L3: a process of this user that made itself non-inspectable cannot be matched: one that predates the run is skipped and counted in the run record, and one started during the run fails the cleanup, so such a run is not concludable.\n\n\n### Follow-ups\nNone.\n\n\n\n--- ENV ---\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-ready\n\n--- STATUS ---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff --unified=3 ddb770352f35f2f55c740e9a8479686de3a251e5..5e72db46c8d8151179ce84a0078ccba16e519cfb -- pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim_linux.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release.go'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff --unified=3 ddb770352f35f2f55c740e9a8479686de3a251e5..5e72db46c8d8151179ce84a0078ccba16e519cfb -- pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim_linux.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release.go'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..610d3cf6\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,305 @@\n+package pose\n+\n+// Releasing an abandoned attempt (spec\n+// pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R5): a dispatch that\n+// ends after reserving its attempt but before recording its run leaves a\n+// reservation without a receipt. Every later dispatch of the bundle refuses\n+// on it, and cancellation refused it because nothing runs. Once it is proven\n+// abandoned, the attempt is closed by the same records a failed run writes:\n+// a failed run naming the abandonment and its receipt. Nothing is deleted\n+// from the ledger.\n+//\n+// Proof of abandonment: a marker that is stalled with its owner proven dead.\n+// A reservation an earlier engine left without any marker (it reserved\n+// before marking) records no owner, so nothing proves it abandoned: it is\n+// listed once it is older than reviewOrphanReservationAge and has no\n+// receipt or run, and released only on the operator's explicit confirmation,\n+// which the recorded run states (review round 3, N2). One release runs at a\n+// time per attempt under a lock the operating system releases when its\n+// holder ends, so an interrupted release leaves nothing to take over (review\n+// round 5, N1). A release is idempotent: it completes with a run the\n+// attempt already recorded, whatever wrote it (N3), and with a receipt\n+// already published it only removes the stalled marker (N2).\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"errors\"\n+\t\"fmt\"\n+\t\"io\"\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+)\n+\n+// ErrAttemptNotAbandoned means the attempt is running, finished, or not\n+// proven abandoned.\n+var ErrAttemptNotAbandoned = errors.New(\"pose: the attempt is not an abandoned reservation\")\n+\n+// reviewOrphanReservationAge is how old a reservation without any marker\n+// must be to count as abandoned.\n+const reviewOrphanReservationAge = 10 * time.Minute\n+\n+// ReleaseAbandonedAttempt records the failed run of an attempt whose\n+// dispatch ended before recording it, and removes its stalled marker. A\n+// stalled marker whose reservation was never published is only removed; the\n+// returned run is then empty.\n+func (s Store) ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(attemptID string, now time.Time, operatorConfirmed bool) (ReviewRun, error) {\n+\tif !validAttemptFileName(attemptID) {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %q names no attempt\", ErrAttemptNotAbandoned, attemptID)\n+\t}\n+\tmarkers, _, err := s.ListDelegationActive()\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t}\n+\tvar marker *DelegationActiveRun\n+\tfor i := range markers {\n+\t\tif markers[i].AttemptID == attemptID {\n+\t\t\tmarker = &markers[i]\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif marker != nil && (marker.StalledAt == \"\" || marker.Liveness != LockOwnerDead) {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: its owner is not proven gone\", ErrAttemptNotAbandoned)\n+\t}\n+\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t}\n+\tlock, err := os.OpenFile(filepath.Join(dir, attemptID+\".release.lock\"), os.O_CREATE|os.O_RDWR, 0600)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t}\n+\tdefer lock.Close()\n+\tunlock, err := lockSpecTransferFile(lock)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: another release of it is in progress\", ErrAttemptNotAbandoned)\n+\t}\n+\tdefer unlock()\n+\treturn s.releaseAbandonedAttemptClaimed(attemptID, marker, now, operatorConfirmed)\n+}\n+\n+func (s Store) releaseAbandonedAttemptClaimed(attemptID string, marker *DelegationActiveRun, now time.Time, operatorConfirmed bool) (ReviewRun, error) {\n+\tvar reserved *DelegatedReviewAttempt\n+\tif marker != nil {\n+\t\tattempts, err := s.readDelegatedReviewAttempts(marker.BundleID)\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfor i := range attempts {\n+\t\t\tif attempts[i].AttemptID == attemptID {\n+\t\t\t\treserved = &attempts[i]\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif reserved == nil {\n+\t\t\t// The dispatch ended between its marker and its reservation:\n+\t\t\t// nothing blocks the bundle, and the marker alone is removed.\n+\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, s.removeDelegationMarker(*marker)\n+\t\t}\n+\t} else {\n+\t\tif !operatorConfirmed {\n+\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: it has no marker, so nothing proves its dispatch ended; if no dispatch of it is running, confirm with --confirm-abandoned\", ErrAttemptNotAbandoned)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\torphans, _, err := s.orphanReservations(now)\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfor i := range orphans {\n+\t\t\tif orphans[i].AttemptID == attemptID {\n+\t\t\t\treserved = &orphans[i]\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif reserved == nil {\n+\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: no stalled marker and no reservation older than %s without a receipt\", ErrAttemptNotAbandoned, reviewOrphanReservationAge)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif _, err := os.Lstat(attemptReceiptPath(s.Root, *reserved)); err == nil {\n+\t\t// A release that stopped after the receipt completes by removing\n+\t\t// the stalled marker; with no marker there is nothing to release.\n+\t\tif marker != nil {\n+\t\t\treceipt, err := s.readAttemptReceipt(*reserved)\n+\t\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\trun, err := s.LoadProvenanceReviewRun(receipt.RunID)\n+\t\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\treturn run, s.removeDelegationMarker(*marker)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: it already has a receipt\", ErrAttemptNotAbandoned)\n+\t} else if !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, fmt.Errorf(\"the receipt of %s cannot be inspected: %w\", attemptID, err)\n+\t}\n+\trun := ReviewRun{SchemaVersion: 1, Scope: reserved.Scope, BundleID: reserved.BundleID, BundleDigest: reserved.BundleDigest,\n+\t\tKind: ReviewBriefKindReview, Adapter: reserved.Adapter, Reason: reserved.Reason, AttemptID: reserved.AttemptID,\n+\t\tStartedAt: reserved.ReservedAt, ExitCode: -1, Purpose: DelegationPurposeReview,\n+\t\tStatus: ReviewRunFailed, EndedAt: now.UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)}\n+\tif marker != nil {\n+\t\trun.Kind, run.Purpose, run.Label, run.Round, run.StartedAt = DelegationKindForPurpose(marker.Purpose), marker.Purpose, marker.Label, marker.Round, marker.StartedAt\n+\t\trun.Failure = DelegationAbandonedCause + \" (stalled at \" + marker.StalledAt + \"); released by pose delegate cancel\"\n+\t} else {\n+\t\trun.Failure = DelegationAbandonedCause + \" (reservation of \" + reserved.ReservedAt + \" with no marker, left by an earlier engine); released by pose delegate cancel on the operator's confirmation\"\n+\t}\n+\t// A run the attempt already recorded, by its dispatch or by a release\n+\t// interrupted before the receipt, completes the attempt; no second run\n+\t// is written for it.\n+\trecorded, found, err := s.recordedAttemptRun(*reserved)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t}\n+\tif !found {\n+\t\tif recorded, err = s.recordReviewRun(run, nil); err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif err := s.finishDelegatedReviewAttempt(*reserved, recorded); err != nil {\n+\t\treturn recorded, err\n+\t}\n+\tif marker != nil {\n+\t\treturn recorded, s.removeDelegationMarker(*marker)\n+\t}\n+\treturn recorded, nil\n+}\n+\n+// recordedAttemptRun finds the latest run recorded for attempt a.\n+func (s Store) recordedAttemptRun(a DelegatedReviewAttempt) (ReviewRun, bool, error) {\n+\truns, lost, err := s.ReadReviewRuns(a.BundleID)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, false, err\n+\t}\n+\tif len(lost) > 0 {\n+\t\t// An unreadable run may be this release's: the history is unknown,\n+\t\t// and the release does not write another (review round 4, N3).\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, false, fmt.Errorf(\"the run history of %s is incomplete, so an earlier release cannot be ruled out: %s\", a.BundleID, strings.Join(lost, \"; \"))\n+\t}\n+\tvar latest *ReviewRun\n+\tfor i := range runs {\n+\t\tif runs[i].AttemptID == a.AttemptID && (latest == nil || runs[i].EndedAt > latest.EndedAt) {\n+\t\t\tlatest = &runs[i]\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif latest == nil {\n+\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, false, nil\n+\t}\n+\treturn *latest, true, nil\n+}\n+\n+// orphanReservations lists the reservations left without a receipt or a\n+// marker, older than reviewOrphanReservationAge, and\n+// says which reservations or receipts it could not read (review round 3, N3).\n+func (s Store) orphanReservations(now time.Time) ([]DelegatedReviewAttempt, []string, error) {\n+\tbase := filepath.Join(s.Root, reviewAttemptDir)\n+\tdirs, dirsTruncated, err := readDirBounded(base, reviewOrphanScanMaxEntries)\n+\tif errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n+\t\treturn nil, nil, nil\n+\t}\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn nil, nil, err\n+\t}\n+\tmarkers, markerLost, err := s.ListDelegationActive()\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn nil, nil, err\n+\t}\n+\tif len(markerLost) > 0 {\n+\t\t// An unreadable marker may be a live attempt's: nothing is known to\n+\t\t// have no marker (review round 6, N1).\n+\t\treturn nil, append([]string{\"abandoned reservations cannot be determined while an active marker is unreadable\"}, markerLost...), nil\n+\t}\n+\tmarked := map[string]bool{}\n+\tfor _, m := range markers {\n+\t\tmarked[m.AttemptID] = true\n+\t}\n+\truns, runLost, err := s.ReadReviewRuns(\"\")\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn nil, nil, err\n+\t}\n+\tif len(runLost) > 0 {\n+\t\t// Without the whole run history nothing is known to have no run.\n+\t\treturn nil, append([]string{\"abandoned reservations cannot be determined while the run history is incomplete\"}, runLost...), nil\n+\t}\n+\t// A recorded run does not finish an attempt, its receipt does: a\n+\t// reservation whose run was recorded without a receipt still blocks and\n+\t// is released with that run (review round 5, N3).\n+\t_ = runs\n+\tvar out []DelegatedReviewAttempt\n+\tvar lost []string\n+\tbudget := reviewReadBudget(nil)\n+\tfor _, d := range dirs {\n+\t\tif !d.IsDir() {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tentries, truncated, err := readDirBounded(filepath.Join(base, d.Name()), reviewOrphanScanMaxEntries)\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the reservations in \"+d.Name()+\" could not be listed: \"+err.Error())\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif truncated {\n+\t\t\tlost = append(lost, fmt.Sprintf(\"the reservations in %s exceed %d entries; the rest were not read\", d.Name(), reviewOrphanScanMaxEntries))\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n+\t\t\tif IsPublisherStaging(e.Name()) || !strings.HasSuffix(e.Name(), \".json\") || strings.HasSuffix(e.Name(), \".result.json\") {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tvar a DelegatedReviewAttempt\n+\t\t\tif err := readLedgerJSONBudgetIn(s.fsys(), filepath.Join(base, d.Name(), e.Name()), &a, budget); err != nil {\n+\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the reservation \"+d.Name()+\"/\"+e.Name()+\" could not be read: \"+err.Error())\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif a.AttemptID != attemptIdentity(a) {\n+\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the reservation \"+d.Name()+\"/\"+e.Name()+\" does not match its identity\")\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif marked[a.AttemptID] {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(attemptReceiptPath(s.Root, a)); err == nil {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t} else if !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n+\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the receipt of \"+a.AttemptID+\" could not be inspected: \"+err.Error())\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tat, err := time.Parse(time.RFC3339Nano, a.ReservedAt)\n+\t\t\tif err != nil || now.Sub(at) < reviewOrphanReservationAge {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tout = append(out, a)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif dirsTruncated {\n+\t\tlost = append(lost, fmt.Sprintf(\"the attempt directories exceed %d entries; the rest were not read\", reviewOrphanScanMaxEntries))\n+\t}\n+\treturn out, lost, nil\n+}\n+\n+// reviewOrphanScanMaxEntries bounds each directory the orphan scan lists\n+// (review round 10, N2).\n+const reviewOrphanScanMaxEntries = 10000\n+\n+// readDirBounded lists at most max entries of dir and says whether more exist.\n+func readDirBounded(dir string, max int) ([]os.DirEntry, bool, error) {\n+\tf, err := os.Open(dir)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn nil, false, err\n+\t}\n+\tdefer f.Close()\n+\tentries, err := f.ReadDir(max + 1)\n+\tif err != nil && !errors.Is(err, io.EOF) {\n+\t\treturn nil, false, err\n+\t}\n+\tif len(entries) > max {\n+\t\treturn entries[:max], true, nil\n+\t}\n+\treturn entries, false, nil\n+}\n+\n+func (s Store) removeDelegationMarker(marker DelegationActiveRun) error {\n+\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(false)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn err\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, p := range []string{filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\".json\"), stallClaimPath(dir, marker), cancelRequestPath(dir, marker)} {\n+\t\tif err := os.Remove(p); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n+\t\t\treturn err\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn nil\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..849a550a\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_linux.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,218 @@\n+//go:build linux\n+\n+package pose\n+\n+// Leftover reviewer processes, the fallback of an embedder that does not run\n+// the adapter under the review run shim (review_shim.go), which is the Linux\n+// path of the pose command (spec\n+// pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R1, L6): an adapter such as\n+// `codex exec` runs each command in a session of its own, so killing the\n+// adapter's process group misses a command the reviewer left running, and\n+// that command keeps writing into the run's runtime directory while the\n+// cleanup inventory reads it. Every process of the run inherits\n+// SealedCheckoutEnv naming the run's checkout, so the dispatch finds them by\n+// that entry, ends them and waits for them before the inventory.\n+//\n+// Each process is pinned by a pidfd before it is signalled, and its\n+// environment is read again through the pinned process, so a pid reused by\n+// an unrelated process between the scan and the signal is never killed\n+// (review round 1, N3). A scan that cannot list or read the state of the\n+// processes fails. A process of this user whose environment cannot be read\n+// (one that made itself non-inspectable) cannot be matched: one that already\n+// existed before the adapter started is not the run's and is skipped; any\n+// other may be the run's, so the scan fails and the run stays unconcludable\n+// (review round 2, N2).\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"bytes\"\n+\t\"errors\"\n+\t\"fmt\"\n+\t\"io/fs\"\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"strconv\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"syscall\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+\n+\t\"golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n+)\n+\n+// reapSealedCheckoutProcesses ends every process whose environment names\n+// worktree as its sealed checkout and returns a description of each. An\n+// error means the run's processes could not be proven ended within the bound.\n+func reapSealedCheckoutProcesses(worktree string, baseline map[int]string) ([]string, int, error) {\n+\tif worktree == \"\" {\n+\t\treturn nil, 0, nil\n+\t}\n+\tentry := []byte(SealedCheckoutEnv + \"=\" + worktree)\n+\tvar reaped []string\n+\tseen := map[int]bool{}\n+\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(reviewReapWait)\n+\tunreadable := 0\n+\tfor {\n+\t\talive, opaque, err := reviewMarkedProcesses(entry)\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn reaped, unreadable, err\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tunreadable = 0\n+\t\tfor pid, start := range opaque {\n+\t\t\tif prior, ok := baseline[pid]; !ok || prior != start {\n+\t\t\t\treturn reaped, unreadable, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d of this user started during the run and cannot be inspected; it may be the run's and was not ended\", pid)\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tunreadable++\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfor _, pid := range alive {\n+\t\t\tif !seen[pid] {\n+\t\t\t\tseen[pid] = true\n+\t\t\t\t// Only the pid: the name is the reviewer's to choose and is not\n+\t\t\t\t// recorded (review round 7, N4).\n+\t\t\t\treaped = append(reaped, strconv.Itoa(pid))\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif err := killPinnedMarkedProcess(pid, entry); err != nil {\n+\t\t\t\treturn reaped, unreadable, err\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif len(alive) == 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn reaped, unreadable, nil\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif time.Now().After(deadline) {\n+\t\t\treturn reaped, unreadable, fmt.Errorf(\"%d process(es) of the run could not be ended\", len(alive))\n+\t\t}\n+\t\ttime.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// reviewMarkedProcesses lists the live processes whose environment holds\n+// entry, and counts this user's processes whose environment could not be\n+// read. A process that is gone is skipped.\n+func reviewMarkedProcesses(entry []byte) ([]int, map[int]string, error) {\n+\tdirs, err := os.ReadDir(\"/proc\")\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn nil, nil, fmt.Errorf(\"the run's processes could not be listed: %w\", err)\n+\t}\n+\tunreadable := map[int]string{}\n+\tself, uid := os.Getpid(), os.Getuid()\n+\tvar out []int\n+\tfor _, d := range dirs {\n+\t\tpid, err := strconv.Atoi(d.Name())\n+\t\tif err != nil || pid == self {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tmarked, err := reviewProcessMarked(pid, entry)\n+\t\tswitch {\n+\t\tcase reviewProcessGoneErr(err):\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\tcase err != nil:\n+\t\t\tif owner, oerr := reviewProcessOwner(pid); oerr == nil && owner != uid {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue // another user's process never inherits this run's environment\n+\t\t\t} else if reviewProcessGoneErr(oerr) {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tstart, serr := processStartTime(pid)\n+\t\t\tif reviewProcessGoneErr(serr) {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif serr != nil || start == \"\" {\n+\t\t\t\treturn nil, unreadable, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d start could not be read: %v\", pid, serr)\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tunreadable[pid] = start\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\tcase !marked:\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tzombie, err := reviewProcessZombie(pid)\n+\t\tif reviewProcessGoneErr(err) || err == nil && zombie {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn nil, unreadable, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d state could not be read: %w\", pid, err)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tout = append(out, pid)\n+\t}\n+\treturn out, unreadable, nil\n+}\n+\n+// killPinnedMarkedProcess signals pid only through a pidfd opened on it,\n+// after the environment read through that same pinned process still holds\n+// entry.\n+func killPinnedMarkedProcess(pid int, entry []byte) error {\n+\tfd, err := unix.PidfdOpen(pid, 0)\n+\tif errors.Is(err, unix.ESRCH) {\n+\t\treturn nil\n+\t}\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be pinned: %w\", pid, err)\n+\t}\n+\tdefer unix.Close(fd)\n+\t// The pidfd names one process for its lifetime; if pid was reused before\n+\t// it was opened, the new process does not carry this run's entry.\n+\tmarked, err := reviewProcessMarked(pid, entry)\n+\tif reviewProcessGoneErr(err) || err == nil && !marked {\n+\t\treturn nil\n+\t}\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be inspected: %w\", pid, err)\n+\t}\n+\tif err := unix.PidfdSendSignal(fd, unix.SIGKILL, nil, 0); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, unix.ESRCH) {\n+\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be ended: %w\", pid, err)\n+\t}\n+\treturn nil\n+}\n+\n+func reviewProcessMarked(pid int, entry []byte) (bool, error) {\n+\tenviron, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(\"/proc\", strconv.Itoa(pid), \"environ\"))\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn false, err\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, kv := range bytes.Split(environ, []byte{0}) {\n+\t\tif bytes.Equal(kv, entry) {\n+\t\t\treturn true, nil\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn false, nil\n+}\n+\n+func reviewProcessOwner(pid int) (int, error) {\n+\tinfo, err := os.Stat(filepath.Join(\"/proc\", strconv.Itoa(pid)))\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn 0, err\n+\t}\n+\tst, ok := info.Sys().(*syscall.Stat_t)\n+\tif !ok {\n+\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d owner unavailable\", pid)\n+\t}\n+\treturn int(st.Uid), nil\n+}\n+\n+func reviewProcessGoneErr(err error) bool {\n+\treturn errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist) || errors.Is(err, syscall.ESRCH)\n+}\n+\n+func reviewProcessName(pid int) string {\n+\traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(\"/proc\", strconv.Itoa(pid), \"comm\"))\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn \"?\"\n+\t}\n+\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(raw))\n+}\n+\n+func reviewProcessZombie(pid int) (bool, error) {\n+\traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(\"/proc\", strconv.Itoa(pid), \"stat\"))\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn false, err\n+\t}\n+\t// The state follows the parenthesised command name.\n+\tif i := bytes.LastIndexByte(raw, ')'); i >= 0 && i+2 < len(raw) {\n+\t\treturn raw[i+2] == 'Z', nil\n+\t}\n+\treturn false, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d state is unreadable\", pid)\n+}\n+\n+// reviewOpaqueProcessBaseline records, before the adapter starts, the\n+// processes of this user whose environment cannot be read, by pid and start\n+// time: they cannot be the run's.\n+func reviewOpaqueProcessBaseline() map[int]string {\n+\t_, opaque, _ := reviewMarkedProcesses([]byte(SealedCheckoutEnv + \"=\\x00\"))\n+\treturn opaque\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..134417e3\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_reap_other.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@\n+//go:build !linux\n+\n+package pose\n+\n+import \"os/exec\"\n+\n+// reapSealedCheckoutProcesses has no portable way to read another process's\n+// environment; outside Linux the process-group kill is the only termination.\n+func reapSealedCheckoutProcesses(string, map[int]string) ([]string, int, error) { return nil, 0, nil }\n+\n+func reviewOpaqueProcessBaseline() map[int]string { return nil }\n+\n+// RunReviewShim is the Linux review run shim; elsewhere it is not used.\n+func RunReviewShim(args []string) int { return 125 }\n+\n+func parseReviewShimReport([]byte) (reviewShimReport, error) { return reviewShimReport{}, nil }\n+\n+func stopReviewShim(cmd *exec.Cmd) func() error { return cmd.Cancel }\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..e4abead7\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@\n+package pose\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"encoding/json\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+)\n+\n+// The review run shim (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes,\n+// review rounds 6 and 8): a descendant of the reviewer that leaves the\n+// adapter's process group, starts its own session and clears its environment\n+// matches neither the group kill nor the sealed-checkout entry. Making the\n+// dispatcher a child subreaper caught it, but a subreaper is process-wide:\n+// in the dispatching process it also adopted and ended children other code\n+// started there (a detached delegation), and reaped processes another\n+// goroutine was waiting for. The dispatcher therefore starts the adapter\n+// through a shim, the same executable re-entered with ReviewShimArg, which\n+// makes itself the subreaper, runs the adapter, and, once it exits, ends\n+// every process left in its subtree. The shim has no other children, so\n+// everything it adopts is the run's. A timeout asks the shim to stop with\n+// SIGTERM, and the death of the dispatcher delivers it the same signal: in\n+// both cases it ends the subtree before it exits (review round 8, N3, N4).\n+\n+// reviewReapWait bounds how long ending the run's leftover processes takes.\n+const reviewReapWait = 5 * time.Second\n+\n+// ReviewShimArg is the first argument that runs the executable as a review\n+// run shim instead of the CLI.\n+const ReviewShimArg = \"__pose-review-run-shim\"\n+\n+// ReviewShimReportEnv tells the shim that the dispatcher passed it a report\n+// pipe as file descriptor 3, and names the dispatcher's pid. The report never\n+// goes to a path the reviewer can write (review round 8, N2).\n+const ReviewShimReportEnv = \"POSE_REVIEW_SHIM_REPORT\"\n+\n+// ReviewShimAvailable says the running executable handles ReviewShimArg. It\n+// is set by the pose command and by the test binaries that dispatch; any\n+// other embedder starts the adapter directly, without the shim.\n+var ReviewShimAvailable = false\n+\n+// reviewShimReport is what the shim found after the adapter exited.\n+type reviewShimReport struct {\n+\tEnded      []string `json:\"ended\"`\n+\tEndedTotal int      `json:\"ended_total\"`\n+\tError      string   `json:\"error,omitempty\"`\n+}\n+\n+// reviewShimReportMaxPids bounds the pids a report lists; EndedTotal counts\n+// them all. A full report stays far below the dispatcher's read bound.\n+const reviewShimReportMaxPids = 1000\n+\n+// boundedReviewShimReport encodes r within the dispatcher's read bound, so\n+// the dispatcher always reads it whole (review round 9, N2).\n+func boundedReviewShimReport(r reviewShimReport) []byte {\n+\tr.EndedTotal = len(r.Ended)\n+\tif len(r.Ended) > reviewShimReportMaxPids {\n+\t\tr.Ended = r.Ended[:reviewShimReportMaxPids]\n+\t}\n+\tif len(r.Error) > 4096 {\n+\t\tr.Error = r.Error[:4096]\n+\t}\n+\traw, _ := json.Marshal(r)\n+\treturn raw\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim_linux.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim_linux.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..ee3d8b08\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_shim_linux.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,249 @@\n+//go:build linux\n+\n+package pose\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"encoding/json\"\n+\t\"errors\"\n+\t\"fmt\"\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"os/exec\"\n+\t\"os/signal\"\n+\t\"strconv\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"syscall\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+\n+\t\"golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n+)\n+\n+// RunReviewShim runs args (the adapter's path, then its argv) as the shim's\n+// only child, waits for it or for SIGTERM, ends every process left in the\n+// shim's subtree and returns the adapter's exit status.\n+func RunReviewShim(args []string) int {\n+\treport := reviewShimReport{}\n+\tvar reportFile *os.File\n+\tparent := 0\n+\tif value := os.Getenv(ReviewShimReportEnv); value != \"\" {\n+\t\tparent, _ = strconv.Atoi(value)\n+\t\t// The report pipe is the shim's alone: the adapter never inherits it.\n+\t\tsyscall.CloseOnExec(3)\n+\t\treportFile = os.NewFile(3, \"pose-review-shim-report\")\n+\t}\n+\t_ = os.Unsetenv(ReviewShimReportEnv)\n+\twrite := func() {\n+\t\tif reportFile == nil {\n+\t\t\treturn\n+\t\t}\n+\t\traw := boundedReviewShimReport(report)\n+\t\t_, _ = reportFile.Write(raw)\n+\t\t_ = reportFile.Close()\n+\t}\n+\tif len(args) < 2 {\n+\t\treport.Error = \"the review shim was given no adapter\"\n+\t\twrite()\n+\t\treturn 125\n+\t}\n+\tstop := make(chan os.Signal, 1)\n+\tsignal.Notify(stop, syscall.SIGTERM)\n+\t// The dispatcher's death stops the shim the same way as a timeout.\n+\tif err := unix.Prctl(unix.PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, uintptr(syscall.SIGTERM), 0, 0, 0); err != nil {\n+\t\treport.Error = \"the shim could not follow its dispatcher: \" + err.Error()\n+\t\twrite()\n+\t\treturn 125\n+\t}\n+\tif parent != 0 && os.Getppid() != parent {\n+\t\treport.Error = \"the dispatcher ended before the shim started\"\n+\t\twrite()\n+\t\treturn 125\n+\t}\n+\tif err := unix.Prctl(unix.PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER, 1, 0, 0, 0); err != nil {\n+\t\treport.Error = \"the shim could not become the subreaper of the run: \" + err.Error()\n+\t\twrite()\n+\t\treturn 125\n+\t}\n+\tcmd := &exec.Cmd{Path: args[0], Args: args[1:], Stdin: os.Stdin, Stdout: os.Stdout, Stderr: os.Stderr, Env: os.Environ()}\n+\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n+\t\treport.Error = \"the adapter could not run: \" + err.Error()\n+\t\twrite()\n+\t\treturn 127\n+\t}\n+\tdone := make(chan error, 1)\n+\tgo func() { done <- cmd.Wait() }()\n+\tcode, stopped := 0, false\n+\tvar signaled syscall.Signal\n+\tselect {\n+\tcase err := <-done:\n+\t\tvar exit *exec.ExitError\n+\t\tif errors.As(err, &exit) {\n+\t\t\tif status, ok := exit.Sys().(syscall.WaitStatus); ok && status.Signaled() {\n+\t\t\t\tsignaled, code = status.Signal(), 128+int(status.Signal())\n+\t\t\t} else {\n+\t\t\t\tcode = exit.ExitCode()\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t} else if err != nil {\n+\t\t\treport.Error = \"the adapter's end could not be read: \" + err.Error()\n+\t\t}\n+\tcase <-stop:\n+\t\tstopped, code = true, 128+int(syscall.SIGTERM)\n+\t}\n+\tended, err := reapShimSubtree()\n+\treport.Ended = ended\n+\tif err != nil && report.Error == \"\" {\n+\t\treport.Error = err.Error()\n+\t}\n+\twrite()\n+\tif stopped {\n+\t\treturn code\n+\t}\n+\t// An adapter ended by a signal is reported as ended by it: the shim ends\n+\t// itself the same way, so the dispatcher reads what it would have read\n+\t// without the shim.\n+\tif signaled != 0 {\n+\t\tsignal.Reset(signaled)\n+\t\t_ = syscall.Kill(os.Getpid(), signaled)\n+\t\ttime.Sleep(time.Second)\n+\t}\n+\treturn code\n+}\n+\n+// stopReviewShim asks a running shim to end its subtree and exit.\n+func stopReviewShim(cmd *exec.Cmd) func() error {\n+\treturn func() error { return cmd.Process.Signal(syscall.SIGTERM) }\n+}\n+\n+// reapShimSubtree ends and reaps every child of the shim, including the\n+// orphans its subreaper status adopted, until none is left.\n+func reapShimSubtree() ([]string, error) {\n+\tself := os.Getpid()\n+\tvar ended []string\n+\tseen := map[int]bool{}\n+\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(reviewReapWait)\n+\t// A process that cannot be inspected or ended is not a reason to stop:\n+\t// the others are still ended, and the problem is reported at the end\n+\t// (review round 9, N3).\n+\tvar problems []string\n+\tnote := func(err error) {\n+\t\tif len(problems) < 8 {\n+\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, err.Error())\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tchildren := 0\n+\t\tproblems = problems[:0]\n+\t\tdirs, err := os.ReadDir(\"/proc\")\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\tnote(fmt.Errorf(\"the run's processes could not be listed: %w\", err))\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfor _, d := range dirs {\n+\t\t\tpid, err := strconv.Atoi(d.Name())\n+\t\t\tif err != nil || pid == self {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tst, err := readShimProcStat(pid)\n+\t\t\tif reviewProcessGoneErr(err) {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\t\t// A process of another user is never the run's: with procfs\n+\t\t\t\t// mounted hidepid it is visible and unreadable (review round 10,\n+\t\t\t\t// N1). Only one of this user is an unknown that may be the run's.\n+\t\t\t\tif owner, oerr := reviewProcessOwner(pid); reviewProcessGoneErr(oerr) || oerr == nil && owner != os.Getuid() {\n+\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t\tchildren++\n+\t\t\t\tnote(fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be inspected: %w\", pid, err))\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif st.ppid != self {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tchildren++\n+\t\t\tif !st.zombie {\n+\t\t\t\t// A pid is reported ended only once its signal was delivered\n+\t\t\t\t// (review round 10, N3).\n+\t\t\t\tif err := killPinnedChild(pid, self); err != nil {\n+\t\t\t\t\tnote(err)\n+\t\t\t\t} else if !seen[pid] {\n+\t\t\t\t\tseen[pid] = true\n+\t\t\t\t\tended = append(ended, strconv.Itoa(pid))\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfor {\n+\t\t\tpid, err := unix.Wait4(-1, nil, unix.WNOHANG, nil)\n+\t\t\tif pid <= 0 || err != nil {\n+\t\t\t\tbreak\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif children == 0 && len(problems) == 0 {\n+\t\t\treturn ended, nil\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif time.Now().After(deadline) {\n+\t\t\treturn ended, fmt.Errorf(\"%d process(es) of the run could not be ended: %s\", children, strings.Join(problems, \"; \"))\n+\t\t}\n+\t\ttime.Sleep(20 * time.Millisecond)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+type shimProcStat struct {\n+\tppid   int\n+\tzombie bool\n+}\n+\n+func readShimProcStat(pid int) (shimProcStat, error) {\n+\traw, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\")\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn shimProcStat{}, err\n+\t}\n+\ttext := string(raw)\n+\tend := strings.LastIndexByte(text, ')')\n+\tif end < 0 {\n+\t\treturn shimProcStat{}, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d stat is unreadable\", pid)\n+\t}\n+\tf := strings.Fields(text[end+1:])\n+\tif len(f) < 2 {\n+\t\treturn shimProcStat{}, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d stat is unreadable\", pid)\n+\t}\n+\tppid, err := strconv.Atoi(f[1])\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn shimProcStat{}, fmt.Errorf(\"process %d stat is unreadable\", pid)\n+\t}\n+\treturn shimProcStat{ppid: ppid, zombie: f[0] == \"Z\"}, nil\n+}\n+\n+// killPinnedChild signals pid through a pidfd while it is still a child of\n+// parent: a pid reused by an unrelated process is never signalled.\n+func killPinnedChild(pid, parent int) error {\n+\tfd, err := unix.PidfdOpen(pid, 0)\n+\tif errors.Is(err, unix.ESRCH) {\n+\t\treturn nil\n+\t}\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be pinned: %w\", pid, err)\n+\t}\n+\tdefer unix.Close(fd)\n+\tst, err := readShimProcStat(pid)\n+\tif reviewProcessGoneErr(err) || err == nil && st.ppid != parent {\n+\t\treturn nil\n+\t}\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be inspected: %w\", pid, err)\n+\t}\n+\tif err := unix.PidfdSendSignal(fd, unix.SIGKILL, nil, 0); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, unix.ESRCH) {\n+\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"process %d could not be ended: %w\", pid, err)\n+\t}\n+\treturn nil\n+}\n+\n+// parseReviewShimReport reads the report the shim wrote to its pipe.\n+func parseReviewShimReport(raw []byte) (reviewShimReport, error) {\n+\tvar r reviewShimReport\n+\tif len(raw) == 0 {\n+\t\treturn r, fmt.Errorf(\"the review shim left no report\")\n+\t}\n+\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &r); err != nil {\n+\t\treturn r, fmt.Errorf(\"the review shim report is unreadable: %w\", err)\n+\t}\n+\treturn r, nil\n+}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/workflows/review.md && cat .pose/rules/security.md && cat .pose/rules/delivery-evidence.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/workflows/review.md && cat .pose/rules/security.md && cat .pose/rules/delivery-evidence.md'","aggregated_output":"# Workflow: Review\n\n## Objective\n\nVerify that a change is correct, production-safe, and aligned with its approved scope and specs.\n\n## Preconditions\n\n- Make the final diff readable through cohesive commits or batches.\n- Provide requirement and spec context.\n- Attach implementer validation evidence, including `pose validate` output.\n- Define acceptance criteria and expected risk.\n\n## Execution checklist\n\n1. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` (or `pose_review_bundle`) when bundle policy is enabled; otherwise resolve `pose review-plan <scope> --explain`.\n2. Confirm the semantic scope, patch/tree identity, mapped components and bundle plan digest.\n3. Run required active tools first, evaluate recommended tools explicitly and leave completion-only tools deferred until an attestation exists.\n4. Select the plan's applicable rules explicitly and record them in the review.\n5. Resolve rule conflicts with the most restrictive option, prioritizing security for exposure, authorization, and integrity risks.\n6. Consult `.pose/knowledge/` for relevant handoffs, accepted risks, follow-ups, and decision logs.\n7. Check compliance with specs, contracts, and local instructions.\n8. Review functional correctness and edge cases.\n9. Evaluate security, observability, and performance risks.\n10. Require validation evidence from `.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json` proportional to risk.\n11. Identify regression and rollout or rollback risks.\n12. Run plan-selected assessments, including tech-debt and integration when applicable.\n13. Classify findings by severity and propose objective actions.\n14. **Produce a handoff** in `.pose/knowledge/` when findings result in accepted risk, post-merge monitoring, or deferred work (`pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>`); link it from the review.\n15. Seal the validated subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`; stop if any input is unclassified or changed since preparation.\n16. Issue a final decision: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n17. Record the decision with `pose review attest <bundle-id> ... --plan-digest <sha256> --apply`; signed external envelopes use `pose review attest --envelope <path> --apply`.\n18. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; reject stale, incomplete, conflicting or transitively invalid reused criteria.\n19. Apply closeout only after verification reports `ready-to-close`; lifecycle, state and report refreshes must not change the sealed bundle.\n20. When Contributor Mode is active and review identifies linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or POSE rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal via `pose contribute stage --type enhancement`.\n\n## Required rule selection\n\nAttach this section to the review:\n\n```md\n## Rules applied during review\n- Change type: <feature|bugfix|refactor|documentation-update|mixed>\n- Workflow consulted: `.pose/workflows/<file>.md`\n- Rules selected:\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/security.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md` when knowledge or process changes\n- Rationale for each selected rule: <one line per item>\n- Rules not applicable: <list and explain>\n```\n\n## Recurrence escalation\n\n- Trigger `.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md` when recurring rework is uncovered and the threshold is met.\n- Require `recurrence_rework_uncovered` evidence for the 30-day period.\n- Require explicit links from the specialized workflow to applicable domain rules.\n- Require a formal keep, adjust, or discard decision after the 45-day pilot.\n\n## Minimum domain coverage\n\n- React UI: apply `frontend-react`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Go services: apply `backend-go`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Cluster deployment or infrastructure: apply `kubernetes`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Process, spec, workflow, rule, or report: apply `documentation-style`, `knowledge-governance`, and security when sensitive data is involved.\n- Cross-stack changes: apply every touched domain rule cumulatively.\n\n## Domain review checklist\n\n### Security\n\n- Confirm authentication, authorization, and least privilege when applicable.\n- Verify that code, configuration, manifests, docs, and logs contain no secrets.\n- Require applicable vulnerability and secret-scanning evidence.\n\n### Contracts\n\n- Confirm compatibility of public HTTP, event, schema, CLI, and file contracts.\n- Validate forward and backward compatibility for rollout and rollback.\n- Require a spec update for contract changes.\n\n### Observability\n\n- Verify structured logs and metrics without sensitive data.\n- Confirm that probes, health checks, and alerts reflect real behavior.\n- Preserve enough traceability for post-deployment diagnosis.\n\n### Validation\n\n- Require lint, typecheck, test, and build coverage proportional to risk.\n- Require executed `pose validate` evidence and relevant results.\n- Record environment limitations and residual validation risks.\n\n## Editorial checklist\n\n- Use imperative, actionable instructions.\n- Keep bullets short and avoid duplicated sections.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- Reference explicit files and paths.\n\n## Required outputs\n\n- A review decision with rationale.\n- The inspected effective plan, its component provenance and tool dispositions.\n- An immutable sealed review bundle and separate attestation bound to its exact digest.\n- A completed rule-selection section.\n- Findings with severity, evidence, and recommendation.\n- Recurrence classification by domain and cause with preventive actions.\n- An explicit statement about public contracts and compatibility.\n- Residual risks and safe-deployment conditions.\n- References to executed checks and collected evidence.\n\n## Complete multi-rule review example\n\n```md\n## Review Summary\n- Decision: approved with reservations\n- Change type: feature (Go API, React UI, and Helm)\n- Workflow: `.pose/workflows/feature.md`\n\n## Rules applied during review\n- `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`): verified handlers, context, and error handling.\n- `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`): verified accessibility and explicit loading and error states.\n- `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`): verified resources, probes, and immutable images.\n- `.pose/rules/security.md`: verified authorization and absence of secrets.\n- `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`: verified editorial consistency.\n\n## Checks and evidence\n- `pose validate`: passed\n- `go test ./...`: passed in the backend module\n- `pnpm lint && pnpm test`: passed in the frontend module\n- `helm template` and `kubectl apply --dry-run=client`: passed\n\n## Contracts and compatibility\n- Preserved `POST /v1/storage`.\n- Added optional `retentionDays`, preserving backward compatibility.\n\n## Findings\n- Medium: missing queue-saturation alert; add a metric and alert before production.\n- Low: frontend error lacks request-id context; improve observable UX.\n\n## Residual risks\n- Review did not simulate real cluster load.\n- Increase monitoring during the first 24 hours.\n```\n\n## CI failure interpretation\n\n- Treat `POSE check (strict)` as merge-blocking on protected branches.\n- Treat failed required checks in `POSE validate (strict)` as merge-blocking.\n- Attach pipeline artifacts: `pose-check.log`, `pose-validate.latest.log`, and the POSE report.\n- Classify optional-check failures as quality reservations with an owned remediation plan.\n- Reject changes that violate specs, public contracts, security, or safe rollout.\n\n## Phased rollout for unready modules\n\n- Enforce the current required checks immediately.\n- Use `moduleOverrides` to phase adoption without weakening global structural and required gates.\n- Promote optional checks to required by module with an owner and agreed window.\n\n### Optional-to-required promotion protocol\n\n- Select a pilot domain and map candidate checks with owners and explicit risks.\n- Measure success for four weeks and require a baseline of at least 95 percent.\n- Promote only eligible domains through `moduleOverrides`.\n- Update the validation matrix and quality policy in the same change set.\n- Monitor regressions and adjust rollout without removing established required gates.\n- Update specs and rules when rollout changes merge acceptance criteria.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Resolve or formally accept every critical and high finding.\n- Make the final decision clear and actionable.\n- Support quality and security conclusions with evidence.\n- Preserve approved scope without unjustified drift.\n\n## Reviewer mode\n\n**Objective:** evaluate technical quality and production readiness with emphasis on correctness, risk, and scope.\n\n- **Focus:** functional correctness, spec consistency, regression and security risk, operability, validation quality, and actionable feedback.\n- **Anti-patterns:** approve without sufficient evidence; review style while ignoring functional risk; request unrelated changes; block progress on subjective preference.\n- **Handoff:** state the decision, severity and evidence for each finding, safe merge or deployment conditions, accepted risks, and recommended monitoring.\n# Rule: Security\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for authentication, authorization, sensitive data, external integrations, dependencies, and attack surfaces.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Apply least privilege to resource and credential access.\n- Store secrets only in appropriate secret-management mechanisms, never in code.\n- Validate and sanitize external input for its execution context.\n- Keep personal and confidential data out of plaintext logs and metrics.\n- Evaluate new dependencies for maintenance, licensing, and known vulnerabilities.\n- Test both positive and negative authentication and authorization cases.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Committing credentials, tokens, keys, or secrets in any versioned artifact.\n- Disabling TLS or certificate verification without a formally documented mitigation.\n- Trusting client input for authorization decisions.\n- Executing dynamic commands without adequate validation and escaping.\n- Ignoring critical security alerts without an approved exception record.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run secret scanning on the changed diff or scope.\n- Run the module's applicable dependency vulnerability scanner.\n- Pass the relevant authentication and authorization tests.\n- Complete a review for sensitive-data exposure in logs and configuration.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Delivery Evidence\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide when writing or reviewing any document that **claims delivery,\ncompletion, or readiness**: status reports, handoffs, summaries, module READMEs,\ncurrent-state sections in specs or proposals, and messages that say something is complete.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Claim delivery only with attached, verifiable gate evidence: command and output\n  (`pose validate`, `go test`, `tsc`, `vitest`) or a link to the corresponding POSE report.\n- Use POSE lifecycle vocabulary: `draft`, `in-progress`, `done`, `blocked`,\n  `superseded`, or `abandoned`. Do not invent labels such as `completed` or `100% COMPLETE`.\n- Separate **implemented and verified** work from **planned or documented** work.\n  A plan describes intent; do not present that intent as current reality.\n- Reference the report or evidence that crossed the spec exit gate before using `done`.\n- Convert relative dates to absolute dates and stamp the verification date.\n- Declare exact source-tree artifact actions in the spec and reconcile them\n  against an immutable Git change set before claiming delivery.\n- Treat artifact declarations, Git observations and delivery verification as\n  separate evidence; none proves the others by itself.\n- For typed delivery targets, require a path from reconciled artifacts through\n  a production entrypoint to current structured validation results. A surface\n  requires reachability plus integration/e2e evidence; a composed capability\n  requires integration evidence.\n- Evidence answers for the component it ran in or for a component inside it: a\n  module-wide run covers its subtree, but a result from a directory inside a\n  component does not answer for the whole component. Coverage that rests only\n  on a containing module is reported as `inferred-coverage` — name it as such.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Claiming `100% COMPLETE` or production readiness without a green\n  `pose validate --strict` for every affected module.\n- Publishing contradictory delivery documents for the same scope; reconcile them first.\n- Merging code with completion documentation before the applicable POSE checks pass.\n- Mixing aspiration and verified state in one paragraph without clear labels.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `pose check --strict` for structure and spec status enums.\n- Run `pose validate --strict` for every module the document claims to deliver.\n- Run `pose lint-spec` when the document is a spec.\n- Run `pose artifact-check --strict` when the scope changes governed source-tree paths.\n- Run `pose surface-check --strict` for specs with `delivers` and `pose\n  roadmap-check --strict` before a roadmap claims completion.\n- `pose assess discover --if-stale --update-state` when closing specs to update completeness metrics, technical debt, and reports under `.pose/assessments/` and `.pose/state/`.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence over progress narratives when rules conflict.\n- When pressured to claim completion without a gate, record the real state\n  (`in-progress` or `blocked`) and the remaining exit conditions.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
